hello i'm darren pemberton deputy leader  of stratford-on-avon district council  and lead member for planning policy we  are consulting on the site allocations  plan  to inform decisions about a number of  areas including how employment land is  provided  the work was underway before the  pandemic but it is even more important  as we support the economy  and its recovery from kobit 19.  we are proposing a new draft policy sap  8  in chapter 5 of the plan setting out how  our new approach to encouraging  new employment sites for industrial or  commercial premises will work  so why are we proposing this new  approach  what research technical evidence and  employer feedback  gathered across the district and more  widely in warwickshire tells us  is that there is a significant shortage  of affordable employment land available  this is slowing down the creation of new  businesses and more importantly is  stopping many of our existing businesses  in their growth plans  and their ability to employ more people  simply because they cannot  find affordable sites to move on to the  core strategy didn't  identify new employment sites to meet  the expected level of growth  however these sites are more and more  being snapped up by multinationals in  sectors such as logistics  to support the exponential growth in  online shopping  and so driving much higher rents because  the existing employment land supply  cannot meet the sea change in consumer  habits over the last few years  on the one hand this is great news for  the district because we are getting much  needed inward investment  the real downside is that existing local  businesses are being priced out of local  growth opportunities  in the long term this will create a  lopsided employment base  that will hamper economic growth and  will not maximize sustainable jobs  our new approach is to encourage new  employment sites  in close proximity to existing  settlements and or existing employment  sites  that are subject to satisfying a range  of site-specific criteria  to ensure that the sites themselves are  indeed suitable for development in  planning terms  in addition we are creating the concept  of affordable employment sites  in the same way that we do for housing  we will require developers to provide 20  of floor space as affordable employment  space  with that space charging a maximum of 75  percent of local market commercial rents  so we'd like to hear your views on this  approach have we got it right  do you think it will work at the very  least given the current impacts of  curvid 19 on the local economy  i think it's important for us to be  innovative in our approaches to the  problem  if we don't try anything new we won't  ever know how we can improve things  as your local authority it is our job to  do what we can to support  local businesses employment and the  economy so please as many of you as  possible need to make your views known  details of how to comment can be found  at www.stratfor.gov dot uk  forward slash sapo and the consultation  runs until 5 pm  on friday the 18th of december so please  let me have your comments  thank you
 

